+An encrypted TCP direction or an HTTP/3 request-stream direction MAY retain
+one complete canonical ACK basis, containing its logical stream ID and range
+vector. Canonical vectors are ordered with strictly separated, non-empty
+ranges. An independent canonical complete ACK replaces the basis; an
+independent noncanonical complete ACK clears it. Other frames, including
+partial ACKs, do not change it. No per-logical-stream dictionary map or
+lifetime ACK history is required. New native streams start without a basis;
+splitting a native stream MUST transfer, not reset, its directional basis.
+
+A relative ACK MUST be complete and name the same logical stream as the
+retained basis. Its encoded ranges are canonical positive additions disjoint
+from that basis. The decoder forms their normalized union, checks the expanded
+range count against the same configured limit and u16 bound, and emits the
+identical full `STREAM_ACK` before any Product processing or ingress coalescing.
+The reconstructed canonical vector becomes the next basis. Missing/mismatched
+bases and invalid additions MUST be rejected without basis mutation. Stateless
+decoders and native datagrams MUST NOT accept relative ACKs. Relative encoding
+does not alter Section8.3 negative-gap authority or feedback publication.
+
+Senders MUST use relative encoding only when it reconstructs exactly the
+original complete vector and is smaller than its independent representation.
+They prepare dictionary changes privately, clear the live encoder basis before
+an awaited native write, and install the prepared basis only after success.
+An uncertain write therefore forces the next ACK to be independent if that
+native stream remains usable. This does not relax native framing, cancellation
+or failure semantics. Decoders advance the basis only for a consumed complete
+record, never speculative read-ahead. Interleaved logical streams can cause
+independent full frames; they MUST NOT cause a growing dictionary.
 
